[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Optimizing Flutter Performance: Const Constructors, RepaintBoundary, and ListView"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Building high-performance applications in Flutter requires a deep understanding of the rendering pipeline. In this tutorial, we will explore three powerful optimization techniques: using const constructors to minimize widget rebuilds, isolating expensive paints with RepaintBoundary, and efficiently rendering large lists using ListView.builder."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Leveraging Const Constructors"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Flutter's UI is built by recreating widget trees frequently. When a parent widget rebuilds, all its children typically rebuild too. However, if a child widget is marked as 'const', Flutter reuses the existing instance in memory, bypassing the rebuild entirely."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"// Non-optimized widget
Widget build(BuildContext context) {
  return Column(
    children: [
      Text('Static Title'),
      MyDynamicWidget(),
    ],
  );
}

// Optimized widget using const
Widget build(BuildContext context) {
  return const Column(
    children: [
      Text('Static Title'),
      MyDynamicWidget(),
    ],
  );
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Isolating Repaints with RepaintBoundary"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"By default, when a portion of a Flutter app needs to repaint, its ancestor layers might also be forced to repaint. A RepaintBoundary creates a separate render surface for its child subtree. This prevents changes in a frequently animating or updating widget from triggering repaints in the rest of the application."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"RepaintBoundary(
  child: CustomPaint(
    painter: ExpensiveComplexPainter(),
  ),
)"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Efficient Scrolling with ListView.builder"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"When displaying lists with numerous items, never use a standard Column inside a SingleChildScrollView, as it renders all items at once. Instead, use ListView.builder, which builds items lazily as they scroll into view."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"ListView.builder(
  itemCount: 1000,
  itemBuilder: (context, index) {
    return ListTile(
      title: Text('Item \$index'),
    );
  },
)"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices Checklist"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always enable 'prefer_const_constructors' in your analysis_options.yaml file."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Apply RepaintBoundary around complex animations, charts, or custom painters."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use ListView.builder with an explicit itemExtent if all list items have the same height to drastically improve scrolling performance."}]
